$DIR/main.p:3:1: unknown directive //pu:interupt (did you mean //pu:interrupt?)
    The compiler has no directive named interupt, so this comment would do
    nothing. It is close to interrupt.
    The directives are: blocking, build, export, extern, fpu, inline, interrupt,
    nochecks, noescape, noinline, noreturn, nosimd, panic, percpu, resultholds,
    scheduler, section, stack.
    A comment that is only a note should start with // and a space.
    Go ignores unknown //go: comments, which makes a typo easy to miss.
    Plutonium directives change what the program means, so a typo must not go
    unnoticed.
    Use the spelling the compiler knows:
        //pu:interrupt
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2600" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:6:1: //pu:alloc was removed: kernel code may allocate strings anywhere the allocator may be called
    //pu:alloc is no longer needed: kernel code may allocate strings anywhere
    the allocator may be called. Delete the line; the program means the same
    without it.
    Plutonium asks for a directive only for a fact the compiler cannot derive
    from the program, so that using the language does not need knowledge of how
    the compiler works.
    Delete the line:
        //pu:alloc (delete this line)
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2601" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:9:1: //pu:noreturn is inferred for a function with a body: delete it
    The compiler finds that a function never returns when it has no return
    statement and its body ends in a terminating statement, such as an endless
    loop, a panic, or a call of a function that never returns, however many
    calls away. The directive is only needed on a function without a body,
    written in assembly or C, which the compiler cannot look into.
    Plutonium asks for a directive only for a fact the compiler cannot derive
    from the program, so that using the language does not need knowledge of how
    the compiler works.
    Delete the directive:
        func halt() {
            for {
            }
        }
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2601" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:15:1: //pu:percpu is in the wrong place: it goes in the doc comment of a package-level variable, not of a function
    A directive does something only where the compiler looks for it. //pu:percpu
    goes in the doc comment of a package-level variable, not of a function.
    Go's //go: directives are ignored when misplaced. Plutonium directives
    change what the program means, so a directive that does nothing is an error.
    Move it:
        //pu:percpu
        var v T
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2602" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:18:1: //pu:inline is in the wrong place: it goes in the doc comment of a function, not of a package-level variable
    A directive does something only where the compiler looks for it. //pu:inline
    goes in the doc comment of a function, not of a package-level variable.
    Go's //go: directives are ignored when misplaced. Plutonium directives
    change what the program means, so a directive that does nothing is an error.
    Move it:
        //pu:inline
        func f() { ... }
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2602" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:21:1: //pu:nobounds was replaced by //pu:nochecks
    //pu:nobounds was replaced by //pu:nochecks: nochecks removes the bounds
    checks and the generation checks from the function's body, and marks the
    function unsafe code (language spec section 18.12, Safe and unsafe code).
    Change the name; the function keeps its meaning, with the generation checks
    off as well.
    Plutonium asks for a directive only for a fact the compiler cannot derive
    from the program, so that using the language does not need knowledge of how
    the compiler works.
    Use the replacement:
        //pu:nochecks
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2601" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:27:1: //pu:noinline takes no argument, found "because it is hot"
    //pu:noinline stands alone on its line. Text after its name is not a
    comment, and the compiler would not see the directive at all. Put an
    explanation on a line of its own.
    Go's //go: directives ignore text they do not expect. Plutonium directives
    change what the program means, so they are read exactly.
    Write the directive alone, and the explanation above it:
        // because it is hot
        //pu:noinline
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2603" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:31:2: //pu:noinline is in the wrong place: it goes in the doc comment of a function, directly above it; it is not above any declaration here
    A directive does something only where the compiler looks for it.
    //pu:noinline goes in the doc comment of a function, directly above it; it
    is not above any declaration here.
    Go's //go: directives are ignored when misplaced. Plutonium directives
    change what the program means, so a directive that does nothing is an error.
    Move it:
        //pu:noinline
        func f() { ... }
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2602" for a longer explanation)
